WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:02.430
Hi.

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.429
In this lesson, we will be creating a masked material

3
00:00:05.429 --> 00:00:08.820
starting from two different sets of textures.

4
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.439
OK, well, actually, before that, we'll

5
00:00:10.439 --> 00:00:12.380
just dive into a little bit of theory

6
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.099
on masked materials and textures,

7
00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:16.579
but it will just take two seconds, I promise.

8
00:00:16.579 --> 00:00:17.730
OK?

9
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.109
Let's go.

10
00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:21.789
So first of all, by masked materials,

11
00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:24.629
I refer to the process of masking specific sets

12
00:00:24.629 --> 00:00:27.949
of textures that correspond to various materials

13
00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:30.469
within a single primary material.

14
00:00:30.469 --> 00:00:33.149
So basically, integrating two materials into one

15
00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:36.740
and then telling Unreal when to show one or the other.

16
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.219
And a common method that we will be exploring today

17
00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:40.859
for accomplishing this involves

18
00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:43.579
using the Material Editor to blend textures

19
00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:46.780
with different values using an alpha mask.

20
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.859
And then this mask will delineate

21
00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:53.460
the boundaries between two or more distinct surfaces.

22
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.799
And another technique commonly used for optimization purposes

23
00:00:56.799 --> 00:00:58.700
in games, because it's really important

24
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.219
to hit that 30 or 60 FPS target, implies

25
00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:05.379
the use of the red, green, and blue channels of a texture

26
00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.739
to store different texture data.

27
00:01:07.739 --> 00:01:09.659
These type of image textures are commonly

28
00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:13.260
called packed textures, with the most common packing

29
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.019
convention in modern game engines

30
00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:19.959
being the ORM textures, planning for occlusion, roughness,

31
00:01:19.959 --> 00:01:22.579
and metallic, where the red channels store

32
00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:25.900
the occlusion, the green, the roughness, and the blue,

33
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.099
the metallic maps of a material.

34
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.859
Also, just for you to know, Megascans

35
00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:32.099
differ from the ORM approach, because they

36
00:01:32.099 --> 00:01:35.540
store the displacement map in the blue channel.

37
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.700
I know, they're all a little different.

38
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.540
And normal maps are typically included separately

39
00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.060
in their own texture, because they

40
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.040
need all three channels for the x, y, and z components

41
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.189
of the normal vector.

42
00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:50.390
Even though for extreme cases, the blue channel

43
00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.469
is sometimes repurposed, since it

44
00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:55.189
can be reconstructed from the red and green channels

45
00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:56.870
to make a normal map.

46
00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:58.310
And then we have the alpha channel,

47
00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.030
which is usually left unused in most common packed texture

48
00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.629
workflows for memory efficiency and compression quality.

49
00:02:04.629 --> 00:02:08.069
But it is commonly used for foliage or vegetation

50
00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:10.349
as an opacity mask, for example.

51
00:02:10.349 --> 00:02:11.870
And to be honest, the alpha channel

52
00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.590
is really used for masking purposes most of the time.

53
00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.789
But really, the way a texture is packed is up to what

54
00:02:16.789 --> 00:02:18.030
you're trying to achieve.

55
00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.990
And an incredible example of texture usage

56
00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:22.189
can be found in an environment included in the project

57
00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:23.550
file for this class.

58
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.789
Because this project was designed as an experiment

59
00:02:25.789 --> 00:02:28.669
to build an environment using one major material with one

60
00:02:28.669 --> 00:02:29.830
texture only.

61
00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.509
And the entire scene, I know it sounds crazy,

62
00:02:32.509 --> 00:02:35.629
but utilizes only two main textures, one dedicated

63
00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:39.389
to diffuse and mask maps, and the other one to normals.

64
00:02:39.389 --> 00:02:41.150
And the materials make extensive use

65
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.930
of masking and different UVs technique

66
00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.180
that we will now see in the engine.

67
00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.219
When we first fire up the project provided

68
00:02:47.219 --> 00:02:50.659
with this class, this map will be opened by default,

69
00:02:50.659 --> 00:02:53.419
map 201 underscore 1.

70
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.020
And if we navigate to the back, you

71
00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:00.539
will find the environment I was just referring to.

72
00:03:00.539 --> 00:03:03.099
And pretty much everything in this room

73
00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:06.860
has been textured using one single packed texture.

74
00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.639
Isn't that crazy?

75
00:03:08.639 --> 00:03:10.599
Right, and I think that the best example

76
00:03:10.599 --> 00:03:15.280
to showcase this material is probably this fellow over here,

77
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.909
which is a packed level actor.

78
00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:21.990
Now, a packed level actor acts as optimized container

79
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.830
for all these different meshes.

80
00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.030
You'll have to double click on it in order to access

81
00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.099
the individual meshes.

82
00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:32.020
But since those are instanced actors,

83
00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.860
we don't really have access to their assigned materials

84
00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.300
directly from the Details panel of the viewport.

85
00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.099
We'll have to fire up this packed level

86
00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:43.699
blueprint directly from the Blueprint editor.

87
00:03:43.699 --> 00:03:45.460
So let's do that.

88
00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.580
I'm just going to have the BP Planet Maker selected,

89
00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:53.639
and then click on Edit BP Planet Maker in Blueprint.

90
00:03:53.639 --> 00:03:55.879
OK, this will open the Blueprint editor.

91
00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:59.060
And I will just navigate to the Viewport panel

92
00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.120
to see the different instanced meshes over here.

93
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.599
OK, I will select one of them, like this little guy

94
00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:08.680
over here.

95
00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.879
And now we'll have to go through a few bunch

96
00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:16.439
of submenus to access the material that was

97
00:04:16.439 --> 00:04:18.120
assigned to this instanced mesh.

98
00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.160
So just bear with me, but we'll get there

99
00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.839
and it will be worth it.

100
00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:26.000
So in the Child Actor Component section,

101
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.110
I'll open up the Child Actor Templates submenu.

102
00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:35.370
Then go to Static Mesh Actor, Static Mesh Component,

103
00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.589
Rendering, and we're almost there, Override Materials.

104
00:04:40.589 --> 00:04:42.750
And here you will find the material assigned

105
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:47.660
to this individual instanced mesh.

106
00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.500
So this is a material instance.

107
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:56.370
So we'll have to open it up by double clicking on it.

108
00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.810
And here on the right in the Details panel,

109
00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.689
I will find its parent material,

110
00:05:01.689 --> 00:05:03.970
which is one texture base.

111
00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:08.379
If I open it up, we won't go too deep, like deeper

112
00:05:08.379 --> 00:05:12.819
than this, and break down this material for now.

113
00:05:12.819 --> 00:05:14.300
But I just wanted to show you

114
00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.620
that there is one main texture assigned

115
00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.939
to it, which is a packed texture with different texture

116
00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.389
data for the R, G, and B channels that are used here.

117
00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:26.670
And then we have this normal texture over here.

118
00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.139
So two main textures.

119
00:05:29.139 --> 00:05:31.500
And let's go back to BP Planet Maker

120
00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.860
and see the different materials assigned

121
00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:34.899
to the different meshes.

122
00:05:34.899 --> 00:05:38.160
So if I select this one over here,

123
00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.480
you can see that there is another instanced material,

124
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:43.360
which is MI Compart.

125
00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.000
So if I double click it, well, we find out

126
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.959
that they have the same master material,

127
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.420
which is one texture base.

128
00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.660
And pretty much everything you're going to click here

129
00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.339
is going to have either an instanced material

130
00:05:58.339 --> 00:06:03.540
or the one texture base master material.

131
00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.180
And that's pretty incredible.

132
00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.410
So that was it.

133
00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:12.639
We can go back to our exercise now.

134
00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:14.279
Okay, you can find in the upcoming slides

135
00:06:14.279 --> 00:06:16.439
the steps to follow along with the exercise,

136
00:06:16.439 --> 00:06:19.139
but let's just jump ahead into the engine.

137
00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:20.420
All right, so in this scene,

138
00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:25.029
we can find a mask material in the Masking Texture section.

139
00:06:25.029 --> 00:06:28.790
And if we zoom in, you can see that this material masks

140
00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.910
golden parts and wax wooden parts.

141
00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.069
And what's actually pretty cool to see

142
00:06:34.069 --> 00:06:36.750
is how these two materials react to light.

143
00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:41.029
So you can see that the gold has these bright hotspots

144
00:06:41.029 --> 00:06:46.029
while the wax wood has these clear and sharp reflections.

145
00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:48.829
We're actually going to be building this material

146
00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.790
from scratch in just a second,

147
00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.829
but I just wanted to show you a little trick

148
00:06:52.829 --> 00:06:54.790
because this material came in

149
00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.550
with a pretty imperfect alpha mask texture,

150
00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.110
but we managed to fix it up

151
00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.189
with just switching one little parameter

152
00:07:01.189 --> 00:07:03.029
in the Texture Editor.

153
00:07:03.029 --> 00:07:05.110
So I'm just going to be opening that up

154
00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.389
to show you what we did.

155
00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:10.129
I'm going to be opening the sample masking material

156
00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:14.660
and then the texture over here.

157
00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:17.699
Okay, so if I keep this window overlaid,

158
00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:19.980
I'm just going to be zooming into an area

159
00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:21.620
of the material where the transition

160
00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:24.980
between the gold and wood is pretty evident,

161
00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:29.089
like this golden square over here.

162
00:07:29.089 --> 00:07:31.430
Okay, so in the Compression,

163
00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.550
we have a Compression Settings parameter

164
00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:38.550
that will switch from default to BC7.

165
00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:42.209
So you keep on looking here at this edge over there,

166
00:07:42.209 --> 00:07:43.730
and I'm just going to be reverting it

167
00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.100
back to its default settings.

168
00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.980
Okay, do you see here

169
00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:51.319
all these alpha impurities over there?

170
00:07:51.319 --> 00:07:54.360
Yeah, that's what I was referring to before.

171
00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:55.920
And what we did was basically

172
00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.879
just switching this Compression Settings

173
00:07:57.879 --> 00:08:01.470
from default to BC7,

174
00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:02.769
and that actually fixed up

175
00:08:02.769 --> 00:08:05.819
most of the impurities of this texture.

176
00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:08.079
So that's all I wanted to show you,

177
00:08:08.079 --> 00:08:10.300
is just a little trick built in engine,

178
00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.899
but it's pretty useful to know.

179
00:08:12.899 --> 00:08:16.160
So now we can start creating our material.

180
00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.220
I'm just going to create a material here

181
00:08:18.259 --> 00:08:20.540
in the Content Browser,

182
00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.819
and call it m-underscore-masking-demo.

183
00:08:23.819 --> 00:08:25.519
You can call it whatever you like,

184
00:08:25.519 --> 00:08:28.600
and then opening it up.

185
00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:32.159
All right, so first thing we need is the textures

186
00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:33.440
that build up this material.

187
00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.620
So in the Content Browser,

188
00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:38.200
you can find the wooden textures

189
00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:42.340
under Base Assets, Textures, and Floor.

190
00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:46.100
And we can drag and drop this into the Material Editor.

191
00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:48.580
And then the golden textures can be found

192
00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:52.299
under Megascans, Surfaces, and Gold.

193
00:08:52.299 --> 00:08:56.720
And then what we need is the albedo,

194
00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:00.019
the metallic, the normal, and the roughness.

195
00:09:00.019 --> 00:09:03.159
Okay, drag and drop them into the Material Editor.

196
00:09:03.159 --> 00:09:04.269
Cool.

197
00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:06.470
So the first thing we're gonna need to take care of

198
00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:08.269
is the base color.

199
00:09:08.269 --> 00:09:10.629
I'm just going to be dragging the albedo

200
00:09:10.629 --> 00:09:13.580
of the gold out here, pop,

201
00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:17.139
the alpha mask over there in the middle,

202
00:09:17.139 --> 00:09:21.340
and then the wooden base color down there.

203
00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.039
And this other five for now we don't need,

204
00:09:24.039 --> 00:09:27.500
so I'm just gonna move them to the side here.

205
00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:31.700
All right, so I'm noticing one little thing here,

206
00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:34.299
that this alpha mask is actually the opposite

207
00:09:34.299 --> 00:09:35.500
of what we need.

208
00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:37.139
We will need to invert it.

209
00:09:37.139 --> 00:09:39.580
We would like the blacks to be where the whites are,

210
00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:42.039
and vice versa.

211
00:09:42.039 --> 00:09:42.879
And guess what?

212
00:09:42.879 --> 00:09:45.679
There is one node that is actually pretty useful

213
00:09:45.679 --> 00:09:50.100
to invert a texture, which is called the one minus node.

214
00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.279
So if I drag out of the RGB output here,

215
00:09:53.279 --> 00:09:56.919
and then just type one minus,

216
00:09:56.919 --> 00:09:58.480
I can actually preview this node

217
00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:02.059
by clicking on this down arrow over here.

218
00:10:02.059 --> 00:10:04.860
And you see, it actually creates an inverted texture

219
00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:08.159
based on the RGB output that we just gave it.

220
00:10:08.159 --> 00:10:09.279
And that's amazing,

221
00:10:09.279 --> 00:10:14.389
because now we can just multiply the golden base color

222
00:10:14.389 --> 00:10:17.519
with this one minus node.

223
00:10:18.059 --> 00:10:19.179
And if we preview it,

224
00:10:19.179 --> 00:10:21.500
you can see that the golden parts got applied

225
00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:24.659
to the white parts, which is what we wanted.

226
00:10:24.659 --> 00:10:27.940
And now we can add to this multiply

227
00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:30.580
the base color of the wood,

228
00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.100
which will actually be applied

229
00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:35.899
if you preview it to the black areas.

230
00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:36.740
Amazing.

231
00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:40.259
So just remember that these add and multiply nodes

232
00:10:40.259 --> 00:10:45.259
actually really work like the blending layers in Photoshop.

233
00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:49.000
So whether you apply the add or the multiply

234
00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:52.480
one after the other or vice versa, it actually matters.

235
00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:55.139
And if I had added first and then multiplied later,

236
00:10:55.139 --> 00:10:56.679
I wouldn't have gotten a different output.

237
00:10:56.679 --> 00:10:58.559
So just keep that in mind.

238
00:10:58.559 --> 00:11:02.500
So the add output, I connect it to the base color.

239
00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.559
And here we're still getting our mass material,

240
00:11:04.559 --> 00:11:06.620
even though most of the heavy lifting

241
00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:10.019
will actually be done by the roughness and metallic part,

242
00:11:10.019 --> 00:11:11.200
because they will define

243
00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.720
how these two materials react to light.

244
00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:17.659
As we also already mentioned in the theory

245
00:11:17.659 --> 00:11:20.399
in the second class of this course.

246
00:11:20.399 --> 00:11:22.360
Okay, so another thing that I wanna do

247
00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.240
is just turning off the specular,

248
00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:26.759
because as I just mentioned,

249
00:11:26.759 --> 00:11:30.519
most of the work is done by the metal and roughness maps.

250
00:11:30.519 --> 00:11:33.080
So I'm going to be direct clicking on the specular

251
00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.279
and then promote this to our parameter.

252
00:11:35.279 --> 00:11:37.559
And I know that this now looks a little bit matte,

253
00:11:37.559 --> 00:11:38.960
but we can always amp it up

254
00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.379
after we set up the metallic and roughness maps.

255
00:11:41.379 --> 00:11:44.240
So for now, let's just do it like this.

256
00:11:44.259 --> 00:11:48.289
Okay, so now we need to take care of the metallic map.

257
00:11:48.289 --> 00:11:51.490
And I said map, not maps,

258
00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:56.029
because there is just one metal in this material.

259
00:11:56.029 --> 00:11:57.759
And that's the gold.

260
00:11:57.759 --> 00:12:01.759
So I'm just going to be taking this gold metallic map.

261
00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:05.940
We're going to be using a very useful node

262
00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:11.110
to be masking the golden metallic areas

263
00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:13.710
with the wooden matte areas.

264
00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:17.129
And that's called lerp or linear interpolate node.

265
00:12:17.129 --> 00:12:20.250
So if we press L and left click on the graph,

266
00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:21.690
you can create this lerp node,

267
00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:25.970
which really works like a 50-50 blend with an alpha input

268
00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:29.279
that can be used for masking purposes.

269
00:12:29.279 --> 00:12:34.269
So I'm going to be plugging the RGB to the A of the lerp

270
00:12:34.269 --> 00:12:37.169
and guess what the alpha is going to be?

271
00:12:37.169 --> 00:12:40.409
Exactly what we used here as mask.

272
00:12:40.409 --> 00:12:43.269
And since we're going to be reusing these two nodes

273
00:12:43.269 --> 00:12:44.490
multiple times in the graph,

274
00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:46.610
but I don't want to have a noodle fest,

275
00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:48.809
I'm going to be introducing a really useful node,

276
00:12:48.809 --> 00:12:51.850
which is called name reroute declaration node.

277
00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:53.490
I know it's a bit of a mouthful.

278
00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:56.389
So if I drag out of this output pin

279
00:12:56.389 --> 00:13:01.389
and then type named reroute declaration node here,

280
00:13:01.889 --> 00:13:04.909
we can create this node that actually is,

281
00:13:04.909 --> 00:13:09.120
it's basically like a variable used in the Blueprint editor.

282
00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.039
It basically stores this input

283
00:13:12.059 --> 00:13:13.820
like a variable as I just said,

284
00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.419
and lets us reference it anywhere in the graph

285
00:13:16.419 --> 00:13:18.220
while keeping this graph clean.

286
00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:20.340
So that's what we're using it for.

287
00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:26.710
I'm just going to call it RGB alpha mask.

288
00:13:26.909 --> 00:13:30.750
Okay, so now if we go down here to the lerp node

289
00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:33.110
and I just right click in the graph,

290
00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:36.950
I have this super useful named reroutes option up here.

291
00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:38.470
And if I open up the menu,

292
00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:41.190
I can find the RGB alpha mask variable

293
00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:42.070
that we just created.

294
00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:43.610
I'm just going to call it variable for now

295
00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:46.019
for simplicity purposes.

296
00:13:46.019 --> 00:13:48.279
Okay, and then I'm going to plug the RGB alpha

297
00:13:48.279 --> 00:13:52.179
to the alpha and preview it.

298
00:13:52.179 --> 00:13:54.860
But something is off

299
00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:58.379
because these two outputs actually look the same,

300
00:13:58.379 --> 00:14:00.720
these two previews.

301
00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.980
And that's because we should apply the RGB to the B,

302
00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:07.679
not the A because the A and B inputs of the lerp

303
00:14:07.679 --> 00:14:10.090
are actually different.

304
00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:12.730
So I'm just going to show you a little shortcut.

305
00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:16.429
If I hold control and then hold left click

306
00:14:16.429 --> 00:14:19.830
while clicking on the A input of the lerp,

307
00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:22.549
you can see that I just start moving around

308
00:14:22.549 --> 00:14:26.110
this RGB output noodle anywhere I want to.

309
00:14:26.110 --> 00:14:29.190
So I can just plug it to the B input of the lerp.

310
00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:31.389
And now the preview looks like we want it actually,

311
00:14:31.389 --> 00:14:33.269
we want the goal to be applied

312
00:14:33.269 --> 00:14:36.090
to the white areas of the mask.

313
00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.250
Okay, so we can plug the lerp output

314
00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:41.279
to the metallic up there.

315
00:14:41.279 --> 00:14:43.340
And you can see that it starts applying

316
00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:47.299
this little sheen on the golden parts of the material,

317
00:14:47.299 --> 00:14:49.440
which is pretty cool.

318
00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.490
So I'm just going to expose a scalar parameter here

319
00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:56.809
by pressing one and left click on the graph

320
00:14:56.809 --> 00:14:59.779
and then connecting it to the A.

321
00:14:59.779 --> 00:15:02.220
So this A input basically represents

322
00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.500
the wooden parts of the material,

323
00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.059
which I'm not metallic,

324
00:15:07.059 --> 00:15:08.779
but I'm just going to expose it just in case

325
00:15:08.779 --> 00:15:12.399
if you want to test some stuff out later on.

326
00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.659
Okay, so now what we need to do

327
00:15:14.659 --> 00:15:18.700
is taking care of the roughness maps.

328
00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:21.139
I'm going to be taking the wooden roughness

329
00:15:21.139 --> 00:15:26.620
and the golden roughness.

330
00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:30.059
We're basically going to recreate this same approach.

331
00:15:30.059 --> 00:15:31.820
So I'm just going to be selecting

332
00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:35.059
the RGB alpha mask variable and the lerp,

333
00:15:35.059 --> 00:15:39.899
and then Ctrl D down here to duplicate them.

334
00:15:39.899 --> 00:15:40.899
Then I'm going to be plugging

335
00:15:40.919 --> 00:15:45.039
the RGB of the wood roughness map to the A

336
00:15:45.039 --> 00:15:49.080
and the RGB of the gold roughness map to the B.

337
00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:53.559
Then the output of this lerp node for the roughness maps

338
00:15:53.559 --> 00:15:57.240
I'm just going to be plugging it into the roughness.

339
00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:59.620
And now we start seeing some magic happening

340
00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:02.240
because now you can see how the gold

341
00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:05.200
is actually differently reacting to the light

342
00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:07.159
compared to the wood,

343
00:16:07.159 --> 00:16:09.159
getting this masked effect.

344
00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:13.740
Okay, so now I think that the wood

345
00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.580
might be a little bit too matte.

346
00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:18.360
So in the specular here that we turned off,

347
00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:21.179
I'm just going to be amping it up a little bit

348
00:16:21.179 --> 00:16:23.860
and turning it up to 0.5.

349
00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:26.019
Now we have this waxed wood look,

350
00:16:26.019 --> 00:16:29.289
which I like quite a lot actually.

351
00:16:29.289 --> 00:16:33.029
Okay, we're just missing one last bit,

352
00:16:33.029 --> 00:16:35.350
which are the normal maps.

353
00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:37.750
So I'm just going to select these two

354
00:16:37.769 --> 00:16:40.919
and I'm just going to be dragging them down here.

355
00:16:40.919 --> 00:16:44.440
We could be using the same exact approach

356
00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:48.519
that we did here with the lerp and the alpha mask,

357
00:16:48.519 --> 00:16:51.960
but I'm just going to be showing you a material function

358
00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:56.200
that actually is more effective at blending normals

359
00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:58.120
compared to the lerp.

360
00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:00.000
And I know, I know, we'll be talking about

361
00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.240
the material functions in two lessons,

362
00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:07.039
but I'm just going to anticipate you this one.

363
00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:10.019
So I'm going to be right clicking here in the graph

364
00:17:10.019 --> 00:17:12.660
and then add a material function called

365
00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:17.349
Blend Angle Corrected Normals.

366
00:17:17.349 --> 00:17:21.789
And if I hover over this blue band over here,

367
00:17:21.789 --> 00:17:24.589
it just says that it corrects the normal direction

368
00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:28.029
of a normal map that is overlaid on another normal map.

369
00:17:28.029 --> 00:17:29.750
Okay, what does this mean?

370
00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:34.069
So this node blends one normal change into another

371
00:17:34.069 --> 00:17:36.829
and it actually does a pretty good job at it.

372
00:17:37.329 --> 00:17:39.890
The transition between one normal and the other

373
00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:42.410
is actually nicely corrected according to

374
00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:45.250
our camera point of view, so where we see

375
00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:47.890
where we're looking the material from.

376
00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:51.319
And that's actually not a harsh transition.

377
00:17:51.319 --> 00:17:54.799
So I'm going to be connecting the RGB output

378
00:17:54.799 --> 00:17:57.440
of the wood normal map to the base normal,

379
00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:00.619
and then the RGB output of the gold normal map

380
00:18:00.619 --> 00:18:02.599
to the additional normal.

381
00:18:02.599 --> 00:18:04.720
And the result of that is going to be plugged

382
00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:08.740
into the normal up here.

383
00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:12.079
Okay, so now I'm just going to be doing something.

384
00:18:12.079 --> 00:18:14.099
I'm just going to be double clicking

385
00:18:14.099 --> 00:18:18.900
on this little noodle of the normal that I have this here

386
00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.500
and then I'm going to be zooming into an area

387
00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:24.630
of the material where we have a transition

388
00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:26.309
because I just want to show you the difference

389
00:18:26.309 --> 00:18:28.990
between the maps applied and not applied.

390
00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:32.710
So if I add a shortcut Alt and left click

391
00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:35.849
on the normal input of the material,

392
00:18:35.849 --> 00:18:39.009
I can just unplug this pin.

393
00:18:39.009 --> 00:18:41.470
And now you can see that the normals are off.

394
00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:45.250
And if I plug it in again, you can see that they're on.

395
00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:48.710
It's not actually a crazy difference.

396
00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:51.690
It's actually just highlighting the edges a little bit,

397
00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:54.289
but I think it will be more evident

398
00:18:54.289 --> 00:18:58.940
in when using some stronger normal maps.

399
00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:00.420
I just wanted to show you this approach

400
00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:01.619
because it's a bit more efficient

401
00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:03.279
than the lerp in this case.

402
00:19:03.279 --> 00:19:06.519
Okay, so with some lerp nodes,

403
00:19:06.519 --> 00:19:09.880
we managed to simply mask two different materials

404
00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:14.880
and create this pretty cool gold and wooden mass material.

405
00:19:16.859 --> 00:19:19.059
And now I'm just going to be saving

406
00:19:19.059 --> 00:19:21.180
and applying that to our scene

407
00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:26.200
by drag and dropping it into our preview mesh.

408
00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:28.420
And here we go.

409
00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:30.720
So in this last exercise we just explored,

410
00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:32.640
we used four different image textures

411
00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:34.920
and their base color to get access to the roughness,

412
00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:36.900
metallic, and normal maps.

413
00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:39.359
But as anticipated, you can embed different maps

414
00:19:39.359 --> 00:19:41.940
directly into the three RGB channels,

415
00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:44.259
like in this texture sample over here in the slide

416
00:19:44.259 --> 00:19:46.819
that we also have available in our project.

417
00:19:46.819 --> 00:19:48.400
I am in the masking textures,

418
00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:51.880
RGB channels section of the default map.

419
00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:54.240
And here we can see that these four,

420
00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:58.079
actually six planes have different materials

421
00:19:58.079 --> 00:20:00.349
assigned to them.

422
00:20:00.349 --> 00:20:02.190
But we'll see in just a second

423
00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:05.130
that these six different materials have been created

424
00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:09.299
using one single packed image texture.

425
00:20:09.299 --> 00:20:12.599
So if I select one of them and then in the details panel

426
00:20:12.599 --> 00:20:16.180
open up the material assigned to it,

427
00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:17.940
I can see in the material editor

428
00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:20.299
that we have one single texture sample

429
00:20:20.299 --> 00:20:23.299
with the red channel connected to the roughness

430
00:20:23.299 --> 00:20:26.759
and the blue channel connected to the albedo or base color.

431
00:20:26.759 --> 00:20:28.980
So this means that this one single image

432
00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:33.279
has different maps embedded into the channels.

433
00:20:33.859 --> 00:20:36.500
So let's open this texture down here in the details panel

434
00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:39.059
and up here you can start isolating

435
00:20:39.059 --> 00:20:41.220
the different color and alpha channels.

436
00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:43.359
So I will be turning all of them off

437
00:20:43.359 --> 00:20:46.019
and then I will isolate the red channel for now

438
00:20:46.019 --> 00:20:50.589
to show you that it's storing our roughness map.

439
00:20:50.589 --> 00:20:52.450
Then I'll isolate the green channel

440
00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:56.910
to show that it's probably storing a metallic map.

441
00:20:56.910 --> 00:21:02.029
The blue channel is storing a bump or height map

442
00:21:02.029 --> 00:21:05.869
and the alpha channel as anticipated in the theory

443
00:21:05.869 --> 00:21:08.109
is probably storing a mask.

444
00:21:08.109 --> 00:21:09.529
Just for you to know,

445
00:21:09.529 --> 00:21:12.910
you can easily pack different images

446
00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:14.869
into the individual channels

447
00:21:14.869 --> 00:21:17.589
using any image editing software.

448
00:21:17.589 --> 00:21:20.410
You just need to select the channel individually

449
00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:22.769
and then paste the gray scale texture

450
00:21:22.769 --> 00:21:24.430
you need into that channel.

451
00:21:24.430 --> 00:21:26.190
And yes, I said gray scale

452
00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:27.490
because it's important to remember

453
00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:30.430
that each RGB channel individually

454
00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:34.569
only stores gray scale values and not color information

455
00:21:34.569 --> 00:21:36.529
because the color is actually created

456
00:21:36.529 --> 00:21:39.930
by the combination of the R, G, and B channels.

457
00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:44.099
I'm going to go back for a moment to the material

458
00:21:44.099 --> 00:21:46.839
and I said before that the red channel

459
00:21:46.839 --> 00:21:48.619
should be used for roughness

460
00:21:48.619 --> 00:21:50.759
but this doesn't mean that you have to use

461
00:21:50.759 --> 00:21:52.359
the red channel for roughness.

462
00:21:52.359 --> 00:21:54.539
In fact, I could be easily connecting it

463
00:21:54.539 --> 00:21:57.759
directly into the base color

464
00:21:57.759 --> 00:22:01.720
or I could just be multiplying the red and green channel

465
00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:04.119
and then connect that into the albedo

466
00:22:04.119 --> 00:22:06.440
and then the red channel to the roughness

467
00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:10.299
and the B to the metallic.

468
00:22:10.299 --> 00:22:12.640
The combination of these different channels

469
00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:13.859
are really infinite.

470
00:22:13.859 --> 00:22:17.740
In fact, this specific texture was used

471
00:22:17.740 --> 00:22:21.660
to texture all the environment of the spaceship

472
00:22:21.660 --> 00:22:26.119
we saw earlier in the theory class

473
00:22:26.119 --> 00:22:30.440
and that's how crazy powerful these packed textures are.

474
00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:31.920
All right, that was it for the class

475
00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:33.140
and at this link you can find

476
00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:35.460
other extensive documentation and resources

477
00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:37.079
to further expand your knowledge

478
00:22:37.079 --> 00:22:38.720
in mass materials and textures.

479
00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:40.599
In the next class, we will be learning

480
00:22:40.599 --> 00:22:42.019
about material functions,

481
00:22:42.019 --> 00:22:44.099
creating an animated material function

482
00:22:44.099 --> 00:22:46.819
and embedding it into a massive material.

483
00:22:46.819 --> 00:22:48.220
Pretty neat.

484
00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:49.779
Have fun building some mass materials

485
00:22:49.779 --> 00:22:51.019
and I'll see you soon.

486
00:22:51.019 --> 00:22:51.839
Bye.
